----- stdout -----
Check for known NAILS traces (evidence, not proof)

Usage: nails verify [OPTIONS]

Options:
  -c, --config <PATH>
          Path to the configuration file (overrides binary-relative discovery)

      --deep
          Perform deep scan (slower, more thorough)

      --offline
          Scan a read-only mounted decoy image or partition instead of the live host. Requires `--root`; live checks (processes, mounts, block devices) are skipped and every path is read from under the image root

  -v, --verbose...
          More diagnostics on stderr: -v for debug, -vv for trace

  -q, --quiet
          Print only errors and the final result

      --root <MOUNT>
          Root of the mounted decoy image to scan in `--offline` mode

      --color <WHEN>
          When to use colour

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal, TERM is not `dumb` and NO_COLOR is unset or empty
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour

          [default: auto]

      --examiner
          Also report setup traces an examiner looks for (encryption headers, containers, crypttab, LVM archives, tooling and history), grouped by what they suggest. Reports only; changes nothing. Needs `--offline`

      --ascii
          Use ASCII markers instead of Unicode symbols and box drawing

      --disk <DEVICE>
          The raw disk or disk image behind `--root`, read-only, for the partition-level examiner checks (LUKS headers, random-looking space)

      --json
          Print machine-readable JSON on stdout

      --no-log-file
          Do not write the log file on the hidden volume (diagnostics still go to stderr)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

EXAMPLES:
  nails verify                        Scan the live host for NAILS artifacts
  nails verify --deep                 Slower, more thorough scan
  nails verify --offline --root /mnt  Scan a mounted decoy image
  nails verify --offline --root /mnt --examiner --disk /dev/sdb
                                      Add the examiner's view of setup traces

----- stderr -----
